89.	 Herpes Zoster Infections 
This is an acute painful blistering viral infection of the skin. It can 
occur in childhood but is much more common in adults, especially the 
elderly, sick or immunosuppressed.   The primary infection presents as 
chickenpox (varicella), usually during childhood. Like herpes simplex, the 
virus persists usually in the anterior horn cells before it is reactivated. Post-
herpetic neuralgia is a common complication and defined as persistence 
or recurrence of pain more than a month after the onset of shingles.
