272
rings, a map-like pattern or giant patches. The surface wheals may be 
accompanied by deeper swelling of mucous membrane such as the 
eyelids, lips, hands and other parts of the body. The deeper swelling is 
called angioedema.
Urticaria is classified as acute urticaria if the condition lasts less 
than six (6) weeks and chronic urticaria if it lasts more than six (6) weeks. 
Causes of acute urticarias can easily be identified as against those for 
chronic urticaria.  
Causes 
yy
Medicines e.g. penicillins, cephalosporins, aspirin, NSAIDS, toxoids, 
animal sera, morphine, radiocontrast media, ACE inhibitors
yy
Foods e.g. fish, nuts, eggs, chocolate, shellfish, pork, spices, milk, 
cheese, food dyes, additives
yy
Infections e.g. sepsis, hepatitis (viral)
yy
Latex e.g. gloves, medical equipment
yy
Medical 
conditions 
e.g. 
lupus 
erythematosus, 
lymphoma, 
polycythaemia
yy
Plants
yy
Idiopathic 
yy
Environmental and physical factors
yy
Pressure urticaria: wheals appear on the area of skin experienc­
ing any form of pressure e.g. soles of feet after prolong standing
yy
Solar, cold and heat urticaria: wheals appear when exposed to 
these environments
yy
Aquagenic urticaria: exposure to water leads to itchy wheals
yy
Cholinergic urticaria: intense pruritic wheals are seen in re­
sponse to sweating, exercise, emotions, and hot foods
yy
Dermographism: linear wheals noticed as a result of scratching 
or pressure
