98.	 Diabetes Mellitus
Diabetes mellitus is characterised by persistently elevated blood 
glucose levels. If left untreated or improperly treated for long periods this 
will result in widespread blood vessel damage and complications relating 
to the eyes, kidneys, heart, brain and nerves. There is an increased risk 
of lower limb amputations, poor fertility and pregnancy outcomes with 
improper glucose control.
Although children and adolescents often present with acute 
symptoms, many adults with diabetes are asymptomatic. It is therefore 
necessary to exclude diabetes in all persons, especially adults, attending 
health facilities for routine medical examinations, first out-patient review, 
ante-natal care, elective and emergency admissions or undergoing 
surgical procedures. 
The non-pharmacological and pharmacological interventions in 
diabetes management are usually life-long.
Individuals found to have venous or capillary plasma glucose levels 
in the pre-diabetes or diabetes ranges, as shown in the table below, will 
need further assessment to confirm the diagnosis.
