Four forms of diabetes are encountered in practice:
yy
Type 1 diabetes 
yy
Type 2 diabetes 
yy
Gestational diabetes (See section on ‘Diabetes in Pregnancy’) 
yy
Secondary diabetes (related to medication use, endocrine or 
pancreatic disease etc.)
Causes 
yy
Autoimmune disorder (Type 1 diabetes)
yy
Idiopathic (Type 1 diabetes)
yy
Genetic factors causing a defect in the action or secretion of insulin 
(Type 2 diabetes)
yy
Environmental factors e.g. excessive calorie intake and lack of 
physical activity (Type 2 diabetes)
yy
Pregnancy (Gestational diabetes)
yy
Secondary diabetes:
yy
Medication e.g. corticosteroid use or abuse 
yy
Pancreatic disease or pancreatectomy
yy
Endocrine disorders e.g. Cushing’s syndrome, acromegaly etc. 
Symptoms
yy
Usually none in many adults
yy
Polyuria and nocturia
yy
Polydipsia 
yy
Unexplained weight loss
yy
Blurred vision
yy
Recurrent boils
yy
Recurrent pruritus vulvae
yy
Erectile dysfunction 
yy
Symptoms related to chronic complications (e.g. ‘pins and needles’ 
sensation or numbness in the hands or feet, foot gangrene, poor 
vision etc.) 
yy
Delivery of large babies (> 4 kg)
Signs
yy
Usually none in most patients
yy
Lack of sensation in the feet or hands
yy
Foot gangrene
yy
Pedal oedema
yy
Impaired visual acuity
yy
Cataract
yy
Retinal changes on fundoscopy
