290
Non-pharmacological treatment
Box 12-1:  Therapeutic lifestyle modification
Diet:
yy
Complex carbohydrates (e.g. kenkey, yam, plantain etc.) are preferred 
yy
Avoid refined sugars as in soft drinks, or adding sugar to beverages. Artifi­
cial sweeteners and ‘diet’ soft drinks, which do not contain glucose, may 
however be used 
yy
A day’s diet must generally consist of;
yy
Carbohydrates (60%), protein (15%) and fat (20%) mostly of plant-origin 
and low in animal fat
yy
Salt (sodium chloride) < 5 g/day
yy
Fruits and vegetables ≥ 400 g/day 
yy
Soluble fibre (e.g. as found in oats, beans, apples, nuts etc.)
yy
A reduced total caloric content (portions) of food and an increase in the 
amount of fibre e.g. vegetables, fruits and cereals
yy
Owing to the special needs of children for a diet that will ensure optimal 
growth, their diet must be determined by a dietician in consultation with 
a paediatrician 
Alcohol: 
yy
Intake of alcohol is prohibited for individuals less than 18 years of age. 
While low consumption is permissible in adult patients, moderate to heavy 
drinking of alcohol increases the total caloric intake and may worsen over­
weight and obesity. It may also increase the risk of hypoglycaemia
Exercise: 
yy
Regular, simple exercise e.g. 30 minutes brisk walking at least 3 days a week 
in ambulant patients 
yy
All advice on exercise must give consideration to the patient’s age and the 
presence of complications and other medical conditions
Pharmacological treatment 
A.     Type 1 diabetes patients and Type 2 patients on insulin 
monotherapy
Note 12-1
Insulin dose requirements vary from patient to patient irrespective of age and 
body weight.
The total daily insulin requirement for most adults and pre-pubertal children 
is about 0.6-0.8 units/kg, which should be given in divided doses. However, it 
is prudent to begin with lower doses and build this up with time to prevent 
hypoglycaemia. 
Insulin requirements increase during infections, puberty, periods of stress, acci­
dental or surgical trauma, pregnancy etc.
The usual route for insulin injections for outpatient diabetes treatment is sub­
cutaneous, administered 30 minutes before a meal for regular/soluble insulin, 
premix insulin, or NPH insulin and immediately before the meal for rapid acting 
analogue insulins (e.g. aspart, lispro) according to the following regimens: 
yy
Twice daily pre-mixed insulin (soluble + intermediate acting insulin) before 
breakfast and supper (preferred) 
Or 

