101.	Treatment-Induced Hypoglycaemia
Hypoglycaemia refers to a blood glucose level below 3.6 mmol/L. 
It is more common in elderly diabetics and those with kidney function 
impairment as well as those on long-acting oral anti-hyperglycaemic 
medications or insulin. Severe hypoglycaemia (blood glucose < 2.2 
mmol/L) may result in alteration of consciousness, fits, self-injury and 
various degrees of irreversible brain damage. 
Following successful treatment of hypoglycaemia, its cause must be 
determined and measures, including patient education and revision of 
anti-hyperglycaemic drug doses, should be taken to prevent its recurrence. 
Causes 
yy
Overdose of any anti-hyperglycaemic medication i.e. insulin or oral 
agent
yy
Antihyperglycaemic medication use in renal impairment and the 
elderly
yy
Omitted or inadequate amount of food
yy
Unaccustomed physical over-activity
yy
Excessive alcohol intake
Symptoms
yy
Dizziness
yy
Blurred vision
yy
Headaches
yy
Palpitation
yy
Sweating
yy
Shaking of the hands and body
yy
Unconsciousness
yy
Convulsions
yy
Irritability and abnormal behaviour especially in children
Signs
yy
Sweating
yy
Tremors
yy
Tachycardia and bounding pulse
yy
Confusion
yy
Unconsciousness
yy
Convulsions
Investigations
yy
Random blood glucose (urgently done using a glucose meter)
yy
Blood urea and electrolytes
yy
Liver function tests
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To rapidly restore blood glucose levels to normal

