Referral Criteria 
Refer all patients who remain outside the target values despite 
adequate dietary, exercise and medication therapy to a specialist.
103.	Goitre  
A goitre is a swelling of the front of the neck due to enlargement of 
the thyroid gland. It may affect persons of any age. Not all neck swellings 
are goitres. Goitres are usually benign but may occasionally be malignant. 
They could be associated with normal, reduced or excessive function 
of the thyroid gland. A reduction in production of thyroid hormones 
results in hypothyroidism while an excess results in hyperthyroidism or 
thyrotoxicosis.  Abnormalities of thyroid hormone production may also 
occur in the absence of goitre.
Proper diagnosis and selection of appropriate surgical or non-surgical 
treatment of benign and malignant goitres is by full clinical assessment 
and investigations. Treatment is not necessarily by increasing iodine 
intake e.g. in iodated salt. Excess iodine intake may actually be harmful 
in some cases.
Causes 
yy
Simple non-toxic goitre or endemic goitre
yy
Hypothyroidism (See section on ‘Hypothyroidism’)
yy
Hyperthyroidism or Thyrotoxicosis (See section on ‘Hyperthyroidism’)
yy
Thyroid neoplasm - benign or malignant
Symptoms
yy
Swelling in the neck  
yy
Breathing and swallowing difficulty, if large
yy
Symptoms of hypothyroidism (See section on ‘Hypothyroidism’) 
yy
Symptoms of hyperthyroidism (See section on ‘Hyperthyroidism’)
Signs
yy
Diffuse (smooth) or nodular (irregular) thyroid swelling
yy
Signs of hypothyroidism (See section on ‘Hypothyroidism’) 
yy
Signs of hyperthyroidism (See section on ‘Hyperthyroidism’)
Investigations
yy
Thyroid function tests – free T3, free T4, TSH'),
('104','Hypothyroidism','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','303','303-304','yy General weakness and tiredness yy Intolerance to cold environments yy Constipation yy Weight gain yy Hair loss yy Dry skin yy Hoarse voice yy Memory loss yy Goitre may be present yy Abnormal menstrual periods and sub-fertility (in adult females) yy Poor growth, development and poor school performance in children','Neonate yy Prolonged neonatal jaundice yy Excessive sleep yy Feeding problems Children yy Cretinism (mental subnormality, short stature, large tongue, dry skin, sparse hair, protuberant abdomen, umbilical hernia, abnormal facies) Adults yy Slow pulse (usually <60 per minute) yy Dry coarse skin yy Puffy face yy Pallor yy Hoarse voice yy Slow reflexes yy Dementia yy Goitre may be present','yy Thyroid function tests - free T3, free T4, TSH yy Fasting blood lipids (for elevated cholesterol level)','Treatment objectives yy To assess and correct level of thyroid hormone production yy To reduce symptoms associated with thyrotoxicosis yy To reduce or prevent obstructive symptoms yy To identify thyroid neoplasms and manage appropriately Non-pharmacological treatment yy Subtotal thyroidectomy where indicated Pharmacological treatment yy Appropriate treatment of hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism (See sections on ‘Hypothyroidism’ and ‘Hyperthyroidism’)','Refer patients to a physician or surgical specialist where complications (e.g. hypothyroidism, hyperthyroidism, breathing difficulty etc.) or malignancy are suspected or if full clinical assessment and investigations are not possible. 104. Hypothyroidism Hypothyroidism is a condition associated with reduction in thyroid hormone production. Thyroid hormone is required for normal metabolism and growth. Its deficiency has major consequences on foetal development as well as intellectual and physical development in infants and children (cause of cretinism). In adults, it may be the cause of several problems including heart disease, menstrual irregularity and infertility, mental health conditions and dementia. Iodine replacement is not the treatment for hypothyroidism. Screening for hypothyroidism in all new borns, and every 5 years in adults after age 35 years, especially females, by measuring TSH (thyroid stimulating hormone) levels in blood where this is possible, should be encouraged. Causes yy Antibody-related thyroid gland destruction yy Subtotal thyroidectomy yy Pituitary surgery or lesions yy Congenital yy Severe iodine deficiency yy Drug induced (e.g. radioiodine therapy, amiodarone etc.)','— Hypothyroidism   —
