At 16–20 weeks
2nd scheduled visit
In sixth month (about 24–28 weeks)
3rd scheduled visit
In eighth month (28–32 weeks)
4th scheduled visit
In ninth month (about 36 weeks) 
During the last visit, the woman should be asked to return 
if she does not give birth within 2 weeks after her expected 
date of birth 
More frequent visits or follow up schedules are implemented to 
meet the client’s individual needs. 
In Ghana, many clients come for their first visit during their second 
trimester of pregnancy. In this case, the visit schedule must be modified 
to ensure that the client receives all the essential components of care 
before childbirth. 
Assessment of the mother at the booking visit
yy
Take detailed history (present health, past medical and surgical 
history, past obstetric history, family and social history)
yy
Confirm the last menstrual period and established gestational age
yy
Examine from head to toe and all body systems including a check for 
anaemia, blood pressure, pulse, general health status and manage 
appropriately
yy
Conduct obstetric examination to confirm pregnancy, measure 
uterine size (SFH) and assess foetal wellbeing (FHR).
yy
Request for/ conduct screening test as per national protocol (See list 
of investigations)
Assessment of the mother at follow-up visits:
yy
Ask about mother’s health status since last visit and ask about health 
concerns
yy
Enquire about foetal activity (gestation > 20 weeks)
yy
Test urine for albumin and sugar
yy
Examine mother for weight gain, anaemia, and for complications 
such as pre-eclampsia and manage appropriately 
yy
Check blood pressure (the upper limit of normal is 140 mmHg for the 
systolic pressure and 90 mmHg for the diastolic pressure). 
yy
Obstetric examination:
yy
Measure Uterine size: measure symphysio-fundal height in cen­
timetres between 20 and 36 weeks gestation.
yy
Check the lie, presentation and position of the baby and the 
descent of the presenting part (3rd trimester). Check the foetal 
heart sounds. Normal rate is 120-160 beats per minute

— Antenatal Care  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
316
Investigations
yy
Full blood count (at booking, 28 and 36 weeks or more frequently if 
indicated)
yy
Fasting blood glucose must be done on all pregnant women (at 
booking, 28 and 32 weeks) See note below
yy
Haemoglobin level (at booking, 28 and 36 weeks or more frequently 
if indicated)
yy
Blood film for malaria parasites
yy
Sickling (if necessary Hb electrophoresis)
yy
G6PD activity
yy
Urine analysis at each visit (proteinuria, glucosuria)
yy
Stool analysis at booking
yy
Blood group and antibody screen 
yy
VDRL/TPHA/RPR test
yy
HBsAg test
yy
HIV antibody testing (counselling required before testing)
yy
Ultrasound scan  
yy
Early ultrasound scan for foetal age estimation and to exclude 
ectopic pregnancy 
yy
Foetal anomaly scanning at 18-24 weeks is beneficial 
yy
Late screening at 36 weeks (placenta position, foetal well being 
etc.)
Box 13-1:  Screening tests for diabetes in pregnancy
Fasting blood glucose test must be done on all pregnant women at booking and 
also at 28-32 weeks 
Test for urine glucose at each antenatal visit
yy
Urine glucose of 1+/2+ on 2 occasions or 3+/4+ on one occasion warrants a 
full oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT)
Those found to have a normal curve can be tested again later in pregnancy after 
28-32 weeks
Those with impaired glucose tolerance or frankly diabetic curves should have 
treatment
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To ensure good health throughout pregnancy, labour/delivery and 
the puerperium
yy
To detect and treat adequately any existing or potential health 
problems
yy
To prevent anaemia and malaria
yy
To ensure delivery of a healthy baby
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Encourage use of insecticide treated nets
yy
Counselling and education on the following topics: 
yy
Healthy balanced diet 
yy
Avoid smoking and alcohol intake

— Antenatal Care  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
317
yy
Avoid non-prescribed medications and herbs
yy
Pregnancy discomforts and management
yy
Malaria prevention 
yy
Family planning 
yy
Breast feeding and new born care 
yy
Immunisation
yy
Danger signs and symptoms in pregnancy
yy
Exercise: Mild to moderate exercise, preferably non-weight 
bearing, at least 3 times per week is to be encouraged. Avoid 
exercise in the supine position after the first trimester. Discon­
tinue exercise when there are danger signs such as any of the 
following: bloody vaginal discharge, persistent headaches and 
or visual disturbances, faintness or dizziness, chest pain and un­
explained abdominal pain 
yy
Exercise is contraindicated in: 
yy
Pregnancy-induced hypertension
yy
Preterm rupture of membranes 
yy
Preterm labour in women with incompetent cervix sec­
ond/third trimester bleeding 
yy
Unexplained Intra uterine growth retardation
yy
Develop a birth preparedness and complication readiness plan 
with the woman
Pharmacological treatment 
A.     Anaemia prevention 
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Ferrous sulphate (dried or anhydrous), oral, 200 mg (65 mg elemen­
tal iron) daily
Or
yy
Ferrous fumarate, oral, 200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) daily
Or 
yy
Iron (III) hydroxide Polymaltose complex, oral, 100 mg elemental iron 
daily 
And 
yy
Folic Acid, oral, 5 mg once daily
Where combination pre-natal drug formulations of Iron, folate and 
other micronutrients are preferred, ensure that the iron content 
meets the minimum requirement dose of 65 mg/dayNte 
B.     Malaria prophylaxis 
(See section on ‘Malaria in Pregnancy’)
yy
Sulphamethoxazole-Pyrimethamine (SP), oral,
5 doses, one month apart starting from week 16 or 1st quickening 
(prophylactic treatment should not exceed week 36).

— Antenatal Care  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
318
C.     Tetanus prophylaxis 
yy
Tetanol, IM, 0.5 ml: 
1st dose from 20th week gestation; 2nd dose 1 (one) month after 
initial dose, if patient has not previously had anti-tetanus Immuni­
sation
A course of tetanus toxoid vaccinations should be given to all women 
according to the following schedule:
yy
TT1-Give the 1st dose (0.5 ml, SC or IM) at any contact with a 
pregnant woman including the 1st antenatal visit 
yy
TT2-Give the 2nd dose at least 4 weeks after TT1  
yy
TT3-Give the 3rd dose at least 6 months after TT2 or during a 
subsequent pregnancy
yy
TT4 & TT5-One dose in each of 2 subsequent pregnancies to make up 
a total of five doses. No further doses will be necessary in subsequent 
pregnancies.
Box 13-2:   High-risk pregnancies
High-risk pregnancies include: 
yy
Bleeding at any time in the pregnancy before labour
yy
Young (<18 years) and older (>35 years) mothers in their first pregnancy
yy
Presence of medical conditions such as: 
yy
Severe anaemia 
yy
Sickle-cell disease
yy
Hypertension
yy
Diabetes mellitus
yy
Heart disease 
yy
Asthma, chronic cough such as pulmonary tuberculosis and
yy
Thyrotoxicosis
yy
HIV positivity
yy
Women with more than 5 children (the grand multiparous mother)
yy
Past history of bleeding after delivery or retained placenta
yy
Abnormal presentation and position of the baby in the womb at term 
transverse lie or breech presentation
yy
Multiple pregnancies
yy
Prolonged pregnancy (when the pregnancy lasts longer than 42 weeks)
yy
Contracted pelvis (pelvis too small for the baby to be delivered safely per 
vaginam). This may be obvious when the mother is short (< 154 cm) or 
has small feet (shoe size < 4½ UK)
yy
Big baby at term when the symphysio-fundal height is more than 39-40 
cm at term or when the estimated foetal weight is 4 kg or higher
yy
Past history of stillbirths or babies who die within the first week of life, 
especially if they die of the same problem
yy
Past pregnancy history of miscarriages around the same gestational age

— Antenatal Care  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
319
yy
Decrease in growth of the baby (foetal growth restriction) - uterine size 
smaller than the gestational age.
yy
Uterine size much bigger than the gestational age with one foetus present
yy
Previous instrumental delivery (vacuum extraction or forceps delivery)
yy
Previous operation on the womb such as Caesarean section, myomectomy 
or when the uterus is repaired after perforation during D&C
yy
Preterm labour (labour before 37 completed weeks)
yy
Rhesus Negative mothers with Rhesus positive husbands and/or with other 
antibodies
yy
Women who are at risk for sensitization must be given Anti-D Immunoglob­
ulin (500 IU) at 28 week and repeated within 72 hours of delivery
Referral Criteria 
High-risk pregnancies (See note above) should be referred to a 
hospital or obstetrician for management and delivery.
 

