('110','Hyperemesis Gravidarum','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','322','322-322','yy Excessive vomiting (sometimes with inability to keep anything down throughout the day) yy Inability to eat or drink (due to fear of vomiting) yy Weight loss','yy The patient looks unwell yy Dehydration (dry skin, dry tongue, sunken eyes in extreme cases) yy The pulse is rapid and thready in extreme cases yy The BP may be low yy Deep and fast breathing in extreme cases','yy FBC yy Blood film for malaria parasites yy Urinalysis and culture yy Blood urea and electrolytes yy Abdominopelvic ultrasound','Treatment objectives yy To stop the vomiting yy To rehydrate the patient yy To treat shock if present yy To treat associated conditions e.g. UTI, malaria etc. Non-pharmacological treatment yy Mild cases can have treatment at home with frequent small meals yy Dry foods such as biscuits may be very helpful yy Drink fluids in sips or small volumes, if able to',NULL,'— Hyperemesis Gravidarum —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
322
110.	Hyperemesis Gravidarum
This refers to excessive vomiting usually during the early part 
of pregnancy, and is usually common. It is a diagnosis of exclusion 
and therefore important to rule out other causes of vomiting such as 
malaria, urinary tract infection, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, hepatitis 
and hypoglycaemia. Surgical conditions such as acute appendicitis, 
bowel obstruction, cholecystitis and twisted ovarian cyst, should also 
be excluded. Hyperemesis gravidarum may be associated with multiple 
pregnancy or molar pregnancy.
Causes 
yy
Pregnancy
Symptoms
yy
Excessive vomiting (sometimes with inability to keep anything down 
throughout the day)
yy
Inability to eat or drink (due to fear of vomiting)
yy
Weight loss
Signs
yy
The patient looks unwell
yy
Dehydration (dry skin, dry tongue, sunken eyes in extreme cases)
yy
The pulse is rapid and thready in extreme cases
yy
The BP may be low 
yy
Deep and fast breathing in extreme cases 
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
Blood film for malaria parasites
yy
Urinalysis and culture
yy
Blood urea and electrolytes
yy
Abdominopelvic ultrasound 
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To stop the vomiting 
yy
To rehydrate the patient 
yy
To treat shock if present
yy
To treat associated conditions e.g. UTI, malaria etc.
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Mild cases can have treatment at home with frequent small meals
yy
Dry foods such as biscuits may be very helpful 
yy
Drink fluids in sips or small volumes, if able to'),
