('112','Pre-eclampsia','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','325','325-325','yy Patients with pre-eclampsia are often asymptomatic yy Swollen feet','Mild cases Systolic blood pressure between 140 and 159 mmHg Diastolic blood pressure between 90 and 109 mmHg Proteinuria of 1+ or 2+ yy Pedal oedema Severe cases',NULL,NULL,'Refer all cases of super-imposed pre-eclampsia or other obstetric complication promptly to a hospital or obstetrician after initiation of treatment. 112. Pre-eclampsia Pre-eclampsia is a disease specifically associated with pregnancy. It usually occurs in the second half of pregnancy and it is characterized by hypertension and proteinuria. The presence of pedal oedema or excessive weight gain may also be a feature of pre-eclampsia. Blood pressure monitoring every 4 hours together with daily weighing of the patient are essential in the management of pre-eclampsia alongside the recommended investigations. While blood pressure reduction is essential, lowering the blood pressure below 140/90mmHg may cause foetal distress and should be avoided. Causes The cause is unknown but the disease is more commonly associated with the following: yy Primigravidae yy Maternal age (women < 18 or > 35 years) yy Multiple pregnancies yy Hydatidiform mole yy Medical disorders e.g. polycystic ovaries, chronic hypertension, diabetes mellitus, kidney disorders yy First pregnancy with a new partner yy Previous history of pre-eclampsia yy Family history of pre-eclampsia Symptoms yy Patients with pre-eclampsia are often asymptomatic yy Swollen feet Signs Mild cases Systolic blood pressure between 140 and 159 mmHg Diastolic blood pressure between 90 and 109 mmHg Proteinuria of 1+ or 2+ yy Pedal oedema Severe cases','— Pre-eclampsia —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
325
yy
Nifedipine sustained release, oral, 10-40 mg 12 hourly
B.     Hypertension in Pregnancy associated with pre-eclampsia and 
eclampsia
(See sections on ‘Hypertension’, ‘Pre-eclampsia’ and ‘Eclampsia’)
Referral Criteria 
Refer all cases of super-imposed pre-eclampsia or other obstetric 
complication promptly to a hospital or obstetrician after initiation of 
treatment.
112.	Pre-eclampsia
Pre-eclampsia is a disease specifically associated with pregnancy. It 
usually occurs in the second half of pregnancy and it is characterized by 
hypertension and proteinuria. The presence of pedal oedema or excessive 
weight gain may also be a feature of pre-eclampsia.  
Blood pressure monitoring every 4 hours together with daily 
weighing of the patient are essential in the management of pre-eclampsia 
alongside the recommended investigations.
While blood pressure reduction is essential, lowering the blood 
pressure below 140/90mmHg may cause foetal distress and should be 
avoided.
Causes
The cause is unknown but the disease is more commonly associated 
with the following:
yy
Primigravidae 
yy
Maternal age (women < 18 or > 35 years)
yy
Multiple pregnancies
yy
Hydatidiform mole
yy
Medical disorders e.g. polycystic ovaries, chronic hypertension, 
diabetes mellitus, kidney disorders
yy
First pregnancy with a new partner
yy
Previous history of pre-eclampsia
yy
Family history of pre-eclampsia
Symptoms 
yy
Patients with pre-eclampsia are often asymptomatic
yy
Swollen feet
Signs 
Mild cases
Systolic blood pressure between 140 and 159 mmHg Diastolic blood 
pressure between 90 and 109 mmHg Proteinuria of 1+ or 2+
yy
Pedal oedema
Severe cases'),
('113','Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','326','326-327','yy Frontal headaches yy Vomiting yy Visual disturbances such as double vision (diplopia), blurred vision, flashes of light yy Epigastric pain yy Decrease in urine production (oliguria)','yy Elevated blood pressure yy Liver tenderness yy Urine production of < 30 ml/hour or < 400 ml/24 hours yy Increased tendon reflexes yy Presence of ankle clonus (occasionally)','yy FBC yy Serum Uric Acid yy BUE and Creatinine yy Urinalysis and culture yy Liver function tests yy Random blood glucose yy Daily assessment of urine proteins yy Ultrasound scan for close foetal growth monitoring','Treatment objectives yy To reduce elevated blood pressure, but not less than 140/90 mmHg yy To prolong the pregnancy as much as possible to allow the foetus to grow and mature for delivery yy To prevent foetal distress yy To prevent or treat any complications that may arise yy To prevent eclampsia Non-pharmacological treatment yy Admit for rest if possible yy Encourage patients to lie on their sides to avoid supine hypotension Pharmacological treatment A. Mild pre-eclampsia There is no need for drug treatment of the hypertension unless the BP rises above 150 mmHg systolic or 100 mmHg diastolic or the patient becomes symptomatic of imminent eclampsia (see below). Evidence Rating: [B] yy Methyldopa, oral, 250-500 mg 8-12 hourly (max. 2 g/day) Or yy Nifedipine retard, oral, 10-40 mg 12 hourly Or yy Nifedipine slow release, oral, 30-60 mg daily 113. Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia This is an obstetric emergency and must be treated urgently. Treatment is the same as that of eclampsia (see below). These cases are best managed in hospital under the supervision of an obstetrician. While blood pressure reduction is essential, lowering the blood — Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia — Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders 327 pressure below 140/90mmHg may cause foetal distress and should be avoided. BP monitoring must be carried out every 15-30 minutes until the BP is reduced and the patient is stable. Thereafter monitoring can be done by 2-4 hourly. Daily weighing of the patient is essential. Symptoms yy Frontal headaches yy Vomiting yy Visual disturbances such as double vision (diplopia), blurred vision, flashes of light yy Epigastric pain yy Decrease in urine production (oliguria) Signs yy Elevated blood pressure yy Liver tenderness yy Urine production of < 30 ml/hour or < 400 ml/24 hours yy Increased tendon reflexes yy Presence of ankle clonus (occasionally) Investigations yy FBC yy Blood clotting profile (bedside clotting time, prothrombin time, INR, APTT) yy Serum uric acid yy BUE and Creatinine yy Urinalysis and culture yy Liver function tests yy Random blood glucose yy Daily assessment of urine proteins yy Ultrasound scan for close foetal growth monitoring Treatment Treatment objectives yy To reduce the blood pressure, but not lower than 140/90 mmHg yy To prevent the mother from suffering from complications of the hypertension such as a stroke yy To prevent fits/eclampsia yy To stabilise the patient and deliver her if eclampsia is imminent Non-pharmacological treatment yy Early delivery of mother if eclampsia is imminent yy If the patient is not symptomatic and the pregnancy is less than 34 weeks allow pregnancy to continue if the foetal condition would allow yy If the pregnancy is 34 weeks or more consider delivery after stabilisation',NULL,'— Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
326
