Daily assessment of urine proteins
yy
Ultrasound scan for close foetal growth monitoring
Treatment
Treatment objectives
yy
To reduce elevated blood pressure, but not less than 140/90 mmHg
yy
To prolong the pregnancy as much as possible to allow the foetus to 
grow and mature for delivery
yy
To prevent foetal distress
yy
To prevent or treat any complications that may arise
yy
To prevent eclampsia
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Admit for rest if possible
yy
Encourage patients to lie on their sides to avoid supine hypotension
Pharmacological treatment
A.      Mild pre-eclampsia
There is no need for drug treatment of the hypertension unless the 
BP rises above 150 mmHg systolic or 100 mmHg diastolic or the patient 
becomes symptomatic of imminent eclampsia (see below). 
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Methyldopa, oral, 250-500 mg 8-12 hourly (max. 2 g/day)
Or
yy
Nifedipine retard, oral, 10-40 mg 12 hourly
Or
yy
Nifedipine slow release, oral, 30-60 mg daily
113.	Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia
This is an obstetric emergency and must be treated urgently. 
Treatment is the same as that of eclampsia (see below).  These cases are 
best managed in hospital under the supervision of an obstetrician.
While blood pressure reduction is essential, lowering the blood 

— Severe Pre-Eclampsia and Imminent Eclampsia —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
327
pressure below 140/90mmHg may cause foetal distress and should be 
avoided. BP monitoring must be carried out every 15-30 minutes until the 
BP is reduced and the patient is stable. Thereafter monitoring can be done 
by 2-4 hourly. Daily weighing of the patient is essential.
Symptoms 
yy
Frontal headaches
yy
Vomiting
yy
Visual disturbances such as double vision (diplopia), blurred vision, 
flashes of light 
yy
Epigastric pain
yy
Decrease in urine production (oliguria) 
Signs
yy
Elevated blood pressure
yy
Liver tenderness 
yy
Urine production of < 30 ml/hour or < 400 ml/24 hours
yy
Increased tendon reflexes 
yy
Presence of ankle clonus (occasionally)
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
Blood clotting profile (bedside clotting time, prothrombin time, INR, 
APTT)
yy
Serum uric acid
yy
BUE and Creatinine
yy
Urinalysis and culture
yy
Liver function tests 
yy
Random blood glucose
yy
Daily assessment of urine proteins
yy
Ultrasound scan for close foetal growth monitoring
Treatment
Treatment objectives
yy
To reduce the blood pressure, but not lower than 140/90 mmHg
yy
To prevent the mother from suffering from complications of the 
hypertension such as a stroke
yy
To prevent fits/eclampsia
yy
To stabilise the patient and deliver her if eclampsia is imminent
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Early delivery of mother if eclampsia is imminent
yy
If the patient is not symptomatic and the pregnancy is less than 34 
weeks allow pregnancy to continue if the foetal condition would 
allow
yy
If the pregnancy is 34 weeks or more consider delivery after 
stabilisation'),
