('116','Anaemia in Pregnancy','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','331','331-333','yy Dizziness yy Swelling of the feet yy General weakness yy Easy fatiguability','yy Mucosal pallor yy Jaundice (may or may not be present) yy Hepato-splenomegaly (may or may not be present) yy Heart failure in severe cases','yy FBC yy Peripheral blood film comment yy Blood film for malaria parasites yy Sickling and Hb electrophoresis yy G6PD activity yy Serum iron, total iron binding capacity, ferritin yy Stool analysis for hookworm ova yy Urinalysis for schistosoma ova and urobilinogen','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms yy To correct haemoglobin level before patient reaches term or goes into labour yy To identify and treat underlying cause yy To recognize and manage the associated complications in mother e.g. cardiac failure, and baby e.g. intrauterine growth restriction Non-pharmacological treatment yy Encourage intake of foods such as red meat, poultry, fish, dark — Anaemia in Pregnancy — Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders 333 leafy vegetables, shell fish, dried fruits which are rich in iron, folate, Vitamins B and C yy Avoid tea, coffee, bran, cola and unhealthy habits such as eating of clay, which inhibits iron absorption yy Encourage intake of folate and vitamin rich foods including beans, avocado, citrus fruits, spinach and mangoes yy Protein rich foods must also be included in the diet yy Ensure these anaemic patients are seen more frequently in the antenatal clinic and their response to treatment monitored with haemoglobin level checks. Pharmacological treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Ferrous sulphate, oral, 325 mg 8 hourly Or yy Ferrous gluconate, oral, 300 mg 8-12 hourly. Evaluate after 4-6 weeks And yy Folic Acid, oral, 5 mg daily yy Blood transfusion Note 13-3 In severe anaemia (Hb < 7 g/dL), blood transfusion may be necessary. In labour severe anaemia is best treated by blood transfusion When there 