kly until third trimester, then weekly until term yy At review visits, enquire about maternal well-being, symptoms of crises, infection and foetal activity. Examine for pallor, jaundice, fever and proteinuria. Check for foetal growth and compare to gestational age Labour and delivery yy Stress and pain of labour and delivery can trigger crisis. Risks of crisis is increased if dehydration, fever, infection, hypothermia develop during labour. Care during labour/delivery is targeted at preventing these triggers yy All women with Sickle cell Disease must deliver in hospital, and the most experienced midwife and obstetrician should be involved in their care yy In uncomplicated pregnancies, spontaneous vaginal delivery must be aimed at until 40 weeks (term). Induction must be undertaken only if the woman has not delivered after this time. yy For the high risk women with recurrent complications deliver as soon as baby is viable yy At admission in labour; yy Labour should be monitored closely with partograph yy Check for haemoglobin level yy Group and cross-match a unit of blood on standby yy Maintain good hydration with IV fluids yy Provide adequate pain relief (See section on ‘Analgesia in La­ bour’) yy Provide prophylactic antibiotics (See section on ‘Antibiotic pro­ phylaxis in labour and puerperium’ below) yy Vaginal delivery should be the aim unless there are obstetric reasons for cesarean section — Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy — Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders 337 yy Prolonged second stage labour should be avoided by offering assisted delivery (vacuum) yy Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) is recommended to prevent PPH Postpartum care yy All Sickle Cell Disease patients must be hospitalized and monitored closely for at least 72 hours postpartum yy Check for pallor, puerperal sepsis, acute chest syndrome, vaso- occlusive crises and urinary tract infection yy Continue with prophylactic antibiotics for at least 72 hours postpartum yy Counsel women and their support persons to avoid crises triggers such as stress, pain, lack of sleep, fatigue etc. yy Encourage early ambulation (especially after operative delivery) yy Review client at 7 days postpartum and thereafter weekly until 6 weeks yy Refer patient to physician for continuing care yy Counsel on family planning and offer contraception Baby yy The baby should be screened for sickle cell disease yy Closely monitor the babies with associated complications e.g. IUGR, prematurity etc. Sickling crises in pregnancy yy Admit all sickle cell patients with sickling and/or obstetric complications during pregnancy to a hospital yy Avoid NSAIDs e.g. Diclofenac in the 3rd trimester yy Opiods like pethidine and morphine for pain management should be used with care during labour due to respiration depression effects on baby (See section on Analgesia in Labour) yy Identify and treat underlying triggers e.g UTI, malaria, dehydration etc. (See appropriate sections) yy Blood transfusion is recommended if haemogloblin level is less than 7g/dl at 36 weeks and/or during crises (See section on ‘Sickle Cell Disease’) yy If in vaso-oclusive crises maintain hydration with IV fluids (crystalloids) 2-4 litres in 24 hours, and provide analgesia (See section on ‘Sickle Cell Disease’) yy Acute chest syndrome (ACS)- foetal monitoring is essential during this crisis (See section on ‘Acute Chest Syndrome’) Pharmacological treatment A. Antenatal treatments (See section on Pharmacological treatments under ‘Antenatal Care’)',NULL,'— Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy  —
