Fig 13-3: Flowchart: management of anaemia in pregnancy
117.	Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy 
Pregnancy in the Sickle Cell Disease (SCD) patient is associated 
with poor obstetric outcomes for both the mother and her baby. Data 
from referral centres in Ghana show that sickle cell complications during 
pregnancy leads to high maternal and perinatal mortality. 
Common pregnancy complications of the mother with SCD include 
miscarriages, frequent vaso-occlusive crises, severe anaemia, recurrent 
urinary tract infections, acute chest syndrome, preterm labour and 

— Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
335
pulmonary embolism. In the foetus, complications include intrauterine 
growth restriction, prematurity and intrauterine foetal death.
Care by a multidisciplinary team consisting of the Obstetricican, 
Physician specialist, Haematologist, Clinical Pharmacist, Anaesthetist, 
Nurse and Midwife ensures good health outcomes for these patients.
Causes 
(See section on ‘Sickle Cell Disease’)
Symptoms
yy
Recurrent Jaundice
yy
Bone and joint pain (recurrent episodes)
yy
Delayed puberty
yy
Sub-fertility
yy
Anaemic complications (easy fatiguability, palpitations)
Signs
(See section on ‘Sickle Cell Disease’)
Investigations
yy
Sickling test and Haemoglobin electrophoresis
yy
Full blood count including reticulocyte count
yy
RDT or blood film for malaria parasites
yy
Midstream urine for Culture and sensitivity at booking and whenever 
indicated
yy
Renal function test (BUN, Creatinine, electrolytes)
yy
Liver function tests
yy
Serum iron, TIBC and Ferritin (if possible)
yy
All other antenatal screening test
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To identify patients with condition early during pregnancy for closer 
monitoring
yy
To correct anaemia
yy
To prevent known crises triggers
yy
To detect and manage promptly all associated complications and 
crises
yy
To deliver client safely as soon as baby is viable 
Non-pharmacological treatment
	
Pre-pregnancy Care
yy
Provide genetic counselling
yy
Assist patient to plan pregnancy in a status of optimum health
yy
Educate on potential risk and health problems of SCD in Pregnancy

— Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
336
	
Care during Pregnancy
At booking 
yy
Assess patient carefully for risk of frequent crises, and previous 
obstetric complications
yy
Examine 
for 
nutritional 
status, 
anaemia, 
jaundice, 
and 
hepatosplenomegally
yy
Perform ultrasound to date pregnancy and exclude foetal abnormality 
and growth retardation 
yy
Perform the above listed investigations to screen for and detect 
complications 
yy
Manage identified problems at booking  (e.g. urinary tract infection, 
malaria etc.) appropriately and promptly
Follow up visits
yy
If low risk, schedule follow up visits monthly until 2nd trimester, then 
2 weekly until 28th week and thereafter weekly
yy
If high-risk, schedule visits 2 weekly until third trimester, then weekly 
until term
yy
At review visits, enquire about maternal well-being, symptoms of 
crises, infection and foetal activity. Examine for pallor, jaundice, fever 
and proteinuria. Check for foetal growth and compare to gestational 
age
	
Labour and delivery
yy
Stress and pain of labour and delivery can trigger crisis. Risks of crisis 
is increased if dehydration, fever, infection, hypothermia develop 
during labour. Care during labour/delivery is targeted at preventing 
these triggers
yy
All women with Sickle cell Disease must deliver in hospital, and the 
most experienced midwife and obstetrician should be involved in 
their care 
yy
In uncomplicated pregnancies, spontaneous vaginal delivery must be 
aimed at until 40 weeks (term). Induction must be undertaken only if 
the woman has not delivered after this time.
yy
For the high risk women with recurrent complications deliver as soon 
as baby is viable 
yy
At admission in labour;  
yy
Labour should be monitored closely with partograph
yy
Check for haemoglobin level 
yy
Group and cross-match a unit of blood on standby 
yy
Maintain good hydration with IV fluids 
yy
Provide adequate pain relief (See section on ‘Analgesia in La­
bour’)
yy
Provide prophylactic antibiotics (See section on ‘Antibiotic pro­
phylaxis in labour and puerperium’ below) 
yy
Vaginal delivery should be the aim unless there are obstetric reasons 
for cesarean section 

— Sickle Cell Disease in Pregnancy  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
337
yy
Prolonged second stage labour should be avoided by offering assisted 
delivery (vacuum) 
yy
Active Management of Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) is 
recommended to prevent PPH 
	
Postpartum care
yy
All Sickle Cell Disease patients must be hospitalized and monitored 
closely for at least 72 hours postpartum 
yy
Check for pallor, puerperal sepsis, acute chest syndrome, vaso-
occlusive crises and urinary tract infection 
yy
Continue with prophylactic antibiotics for at least 72 hours 
postpartum
yy
Counsel women and their support persons to avoid crises triggers 
such as stress, pain, lack of sleep, fatigue etc.
yy
Encourage early ambulation (especially after operative delivery) 
yy
Review client at 7 days postpartum and thereafter weekly until 6 
weeks
yy
Refer patient to physician for continuing care
yy
Counsel on family planning and offer contraception
	
Baby
yy
The baby should be screened for sickle cell disease
yy
Closely monitor the babies with associated complications e.g. IUGR, 
prematurity etc.
	
Sickling crises in pregnancy 
yy
Admit all sickle cell patients with sickling and/or obstetric 
complications during pregnancy to a hospital
yy
Avoid NSAIDs e.g. Diclofenac in the 3rd trimester
yy
Opiods like pethidine and morphine for pain management should be 
used with care during labour due to respiration depression effects on 
baby (See section on Analgesia in Labour)
yy
Identify and treat underlying triggers e.g UTI, malaria, dehydration 
etc. (See appropriate sections)
yy
Blood transfusion is recommended if haemogloblin level is less than 
7g/dl at 36 weeks and/or during crises (See section on ‘Sickle Cell 
Disease’)
yy
If in vaso-oclusive crises maintain hydration with IV fluids (crystalloids) 
2-4 litres in 24 hours, and provide analgesia (See section on ‘Sickle 
Cell Disease’)
yy
Acute chest syndrome (ACS)- foetal monitoring is essential during 
this crisis (See section on ‘Acute Chest Syndrome’)
Pharmacological treatment 
